      title: "Законы, о которых все забыли",
      text: "It is often said that in England it is still illegal to die in the Houses of Parliament, or that a pregnant woman may relieve herself in a policeman's helmet. Neither claim is true, but both are repeated so often that they are widely believed to be genuine. The popularity of such stories points to a real phenomenon, however: legal systems are full of statutes that nobody remembers, nobody enforces and nobody has bothered to repeal.\n\nTake the Metropolitan Police Act of 1839, which is still in force. Under one of its provisions, it is an offence to beat or shake any carpet or rug in a London street, except doormats before eight in the morning. No one is known to have been prosecuted for this in living memory, yet the clause remains on the statute book. In the United States, where laws are made at federal, state and local level, the number of forgotten rules is thought to run into the tens of thousands.\n\nWhy do such laws survive? Partly because repealing legislation takes time, and lawmakers have more pressing priorities. Removing an old by-law about horse-drawn carriages is unlikely to win anyone an election. There is also a degree of caution. A law that seems absurd today may turn out to be the only legal basis for something useful, and lawyers are wary of unintended consequences. In practice, it is often easier to leave a dead law alone than to risk creating a loophole.\n\nMost of the time, this does no harm. Occasionally, however, a forgotten statute comes back to life. Prosecutors searching for a way to charge someone may discover an ancient offence that fits the case perfectly, even though it was written for a completely different world. Critics argue that this undermines a basic principle of the rule of law: citizens should be able to know what is forbidden. If the law is so vast that nobody could possibly read it all, the old saying that ignorance of the law is no excuse begins to sound less like a principle and more like a trap.\n\nSome countries have taken action. In the United Kingdom, the Law Commission has, since the 1960s, been responsible for identifying obsolete legislation, and thousands of acts have been removed as a result. Some governments now include sunset clauses in new laws, so that they automatically expire after a set period unless parliament votes to renew them. Supporters say this forces lawmakers to justify a law's existence regularly.\n\nNone of this, of course, will stop people sharing stories about bizarre laws online. But the next time you read that it is illegal to do something ridiculous, it is worth asking a simple question: is anyone actually enforcing it?",
