      text: "On 10 December 1948, in a hall in Paris, the General Assembly of the newly founded United Nations adopted a document that many delegates privately doubted would ever matter. The Universal Declaration of Human Rights contained just thirty articles, and it was not legally binding. No country could be punished for ignoring it. Yet more than seventy-five years later, it is widely regarded as one of the most influential texts of the modern era and holds the record as the most translated document in the world.\n\nThe Declaration was born out of the horrors of the Second World War. Its drafters, led by Eleanor Roosevelt, believed it was essential that the world agree on a basic set of protections that no government could take away. The very first article sets the tone: all human beings are born free and equal in dignity and rights. Later articles protect freedom of expression and religion, forbid torture and arbitrary detention, and guarantee everyone the right to a fair trial. Remarkably, the document also includes social and economic rights, such as the right to education, to work and to rest, reflecting the influence of delegates from very different political systems.\n\nReaching agreement was far from easy. Delegates argued for months over whether rights came from nature, from God or from society, and the final text avoids the question entirely. When the vote finally came, forty-eight countries voted in favour and none against, but eight abstained, including the Soviet Union and several of its allies. Critics at the time dismissed the Declaration as a list of noble intentions with no mechanism for enforcement.\n\nIn a narrow sense, they were right. The Declaration itself cannot send anyone to prison. But its principles have since been enshrined in dozens of national constitutions and in binding international treaties, which allow citizens in some regions to take their own governments to court. Human rights organisations and watchdogs around the world use its language to document violations and to demand that those responsible be held accountable.\n\nThat is not to say the picture is entirely positive. Persecution, censorship and torture remain widespread, and many governments that formally accept the Declaration routinely ignore it. Some critics argue that the idea of universal rights reflects Western values and is used selectively, as a political weapon against rivals rather than a standard applied equally to all.\n\nEven so, the Declaration has achieved something its sceptics never expected. It has changed the terms of the debate. Today, governments accused of abuses rarely argue that human rights do not matter; instead, they insist that they are respecting them. The gap between what states say and what they do is still enormous, but the standard by which they are judged has been set.",
