      minWords: 250, use: ["turnout", "nevertheless", "whereas", "consequently"],
      model: "In many democracies, election turnout has been falling for decades, and in some cases fewer than half of eligible citizens now bother to vote. One proposed solution is to make voting compulsory, as it already is in Australia, Belgium and several other countries. This essay will consider the arguments on both sides.\n\nSupporters of compulsory voting argue that it makes governments genuinely representative. When only some groups vote, typically older and wealthier citizens, politicians naturally design policies for them. Consequently, the poor and the young are effectively excluded from decision-making. If everyone is required to vote, parties are forced to address the concerns of the whole electorate. Furthermore, voting can be seen as a civic duty, comparable to paying taxes or serving on a jury.\n\nNevertheless, there are serious objections. Many people believe that the freedom to vote must include the freedom not to vote, and that forcing citizens to take part is an infringement of their rights. Critics also point out that compulsory voting may simply increase the number of uninformed or random choices. Moreover, it does nothing to tackle the reasons why people abstain, such as distrust of politicians or the feeling that all parties are the same.\n\nIt seems to me that compulsion treats the symptom rather than the disease. Countries with compulsory voting do achieve high participation, whereas the quality of public debate there is not obviously better than elsewhere. A more effective approach would be to make voting easier, through automatic registration and secure postal or online voting, and to improve civic education in schools.\n\nIn conclusion, although compulsory voting has clear benefits, I believe that democracy is healthier when people vote because they want to, not because they have to."
