    text: "In 2016, ninety-nine Irish citizens, chosen at random from the electoral register, began meeting at a hotel outside Dublin at weekends. They were teachers, farmers, retired engineers and students, and most had never been involved in politics. Their task was to consider some of the most divisive questions facing the country, including the constitutional ban on abortion. Over several months they heard evidence from doctors, lawyers, ethicists and people directly affected, and then voted on their recommendations. Their conclusion, which surprised many politicians, was that the constitution should be changed. Two years later, in a referendum, voters approved that change by a large majority.\n\nThe Irish Citizens' Assembly is often held up as a model of what is known as deliberative democracy. The idea is far from new; in ancient Athens, many public officials were chosen by lot rather than elected. Its modern revival, however, reflects a widespread sense that conventional politics is failing. Elected representatives, it is argued, are under constant pressure from party leaders, donors and the next election, and are consequently reluctant to address long-term or controversial issues. Ordinary citizens, freed from these pressures, can afford to follow the evidence wherever it leads.\n\nSupporters point to several advantages. Because members are selected to reflect the population in terms of age, gender, region and education, assemblies are often more representative than parliaments. Furthermore, the process itself appears to change people. Participants frequently report that they arrived with firm opinions and left with a more nuanced understanding of the arguments on the other side. In an age of polarisation, that alone is considered valuable.\n\nNevertheless, the model has its critics. Some argue that randomly chosen citizens lack the expertise to judge complex technical questions and are therefore heavily dependent on the experts who brief them, whose selection can shape the outcome. Others raise a more fundamental objection: assemblies have no democratic mandate. Nobody voted for their members, and they cannot be removed at the next election. If their recommendations are watered down or ignored, as critics say happened to many proposals of France's Citizens' Convention for Climate in 2020, participants and the public may end up more disillusioned than before.\n\nFor this reason, most advocates do not see assemblies as a replacement for elected parliaments but as a supplement to them. They work best, it is generally agreed, when governments commit in advance to responding seriously to their recommendations, whether by putting them to a referendum or by explaining publicly why they have been rejected.\n\nWhether citizens' assemblies will become a permanent feature of democratic life remains to be seen. What they have already shown is that ordinary people, given time, information and respect, are capable of discussing difficult questions calmly. That, in itself, is a message many politicians would do well to hear.",
